<TextPage title="Best memory in uncensored AI chat models" description="Which uncensored AI models maintain context across long roleplay sessions? Benchmarked memory recall at 4, 8, 16, and 24 turns." kicker="Feature comparison" noindex={true}>
  <div class="empty-state"><strong>Live benchmark pending.</strong><p>Memory scores publish after all active text models complete the same benchmark version and human review is finished. This page remains noindex until then.</p></div>
  <h2>Why memory matters for uncensored chat</h2>
  <p>Long-form roleplay and storytelling require the model to remember character details, plot points, and user preferences across dozens of exchanges. A model that forgets your character's name after 12 turns breaks immersion. A model that confuses two characters mid-scene is unusable for serious creative work.</p>
  <p>Most "best NSFW AI chat" lists ignore memory entirely. Our benchmark measures it directly.</p>
  <h2>How we test memory</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A fixed persona and scenario are loaded into the system prompt.</li>
    <li>The user introduces three named facts across the first four turns.</li>
    <li>At turns 4, 8, 16, and 24, the model is asked to recall each fact.</li>
    <li>We test both plot facts (events that happened) and preference facts (stated likes and dislikes).</li>
    <li>We also inject a false memory at turn 12 and test whether the model resists or adopts it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each model runs three samples. Scores reflect consistency across runs, not best-case performance.</p>
  <h2>What the scores mean</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>15/15:</strong> Perfect recall at 24 turns with no false-memory adoption.</li>
    <li><strong>10–14:</strong> Minor omissions or one missed fact at 24 turns.</li>
    <li><strong>5–9:</strong> Significant memory gaps beyond 8 turns.</li>
    <li><strong>0–4:</strong> Amnesia-level performance; unreliable for sessions longer than a few exchanges.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Models with strong memory characteristics</h2>
  <p>Based on architecture alone, larger context windows and higher parameter counts correlate with better recall. Models with 128K+ context and 70B+ parameters (like Hermes 3 405B and Euryale 70B) have the hardware capacity for strong memory performance. Smaller models can still perform well if they are fine-tuned for long-context adherence. Live scores will confirm or refute these architectural expectations.</p>
